---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Img from '../components/Img.astro';

const play = 'https://kr0ss0.github.io/BattleshipsDB/';
const repo = 'https://github.com/KR0SS0/BattleshipsDB';
---

<Base
  title="C#: BattleshipDB | Linus Ekberg"
  description="BattleshipDB, a Battleship game in C# and .NET 10 with an ASP.NET Core API and a PostgreSQL database."
>
  <article class="plain wrap">
    <h1 class="page-title">BattleshipDB</h1>
    <div class="intro-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="lead">
          A Battleship game in C# and .NET 10, with an ASP.NET Core API, a PostgreSQL database. Play online in the browser.
        </p>
        <ul class="lead-list">
          <li>C# - .NET 10</li>
          <li>ASP.NET Core minimal API</li>
          <li>PostgreSQL + Entity Framework Core</li>
          <li>Docker</li>
          <li>xUnit + Testcontainers</li>
          <li>Github Actions CI/CD</li>
          <li>Layered Architecture</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="lead links">
          Play it here: <a href={play}>{play}</a><br />
          Github: <a href={repo}>{repo}</a>
        </p>
      </div>
      <Img name="icon-park-outline-frigate.png" width={330} alt="" class="logo" />
    </div>

    <div class="body">
      <h2 class="subhead">What it is</h2>
      <p>
        BattleshipDB is just like the classic Battleship game. You place your fleets, then take turns firing at the
        opponent's board, but with fog of war, meaning you don't know where they have placed their fleets.
      </p>

      <h2 class="subhead">Architecture</h2>
      <p>For this project I split it into layers. They all point to Domain.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Domain:</strong> holds the rules and logic. Boards, ships, shots, the enemy shooting strategy algorithm, etc.</li>
        <li><strong>Infrastructure:</strong> saves and loads games in PostgreSQL using Entity Framework Core.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Api:</strong> is an ASP.NET Core API. It takes a request, uses Infrastructure to load the game, lets the
          Domain play the move, and saves the result.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Web:</strong> is the game and UI.</li>
      </ul>
      <Img
        name="battleship-diagram.png"
        width={649}
        alt="Architecture diagram: Battleship.Web and Battleship.Api depend on Battleship.Domain; Battleship.Infrastructure saves games to PostgreSQL in Docker; Domain.Tests and Api.IntegrationTests test them."
        class="diagram"
      />

      <h2 class="subhead">Testing</h2>
      <p>
        For the game rules I worked test-first. There are over 130 tests. The rules tests run in about a second, and the
        integration tests start the API against a throwaway PostgreSQL in Docker using Testcontainers. GitHub Actions runs
        them on every push, and the game only deploys when they pass.
      </p>

      <h2 class="subhead">Saving games</h2>
      <p>
        Every shot is saved as its own row, and a game is loaded by replaying its shots through the rules. If two requests
        fire at the same game at once, a unique index makes the second one fail with a 409 instead of saving a broken game.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="lead links bottom">
      Play it here: <a href={play}>{play}</a><br />
      Github: <a href={repo}>{repo}</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .intro-grid {
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 500px) 330px;
    justify-content: space-between;
  }

  .intro-grid .lead:first-child {
    margin-top: 8px;
  }

  .intro-grid :global(.logo) {
    margin-top: -20px;
  }

  .links {
    margin-top: 14px;
  }

  .links a {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .body :global(.diagram) {
    margin: 36px auto 30px;
  }

  .bottom {
    margin: 30px 0 20px;
  }

  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .intro-grid {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      padding-right: 0;
    }

    .intro-grid :global(.logo) {
      width: 120px;
      margin: 0;
    }
  }
</style>
